**Proof.**

Let $M\subset\mathbb{C}$ be a subfield, closed under complex conjugation, with $M\not\subset\mathbb{R}$. Recall $L(M)$ is the set of lines in $\mathbb{C}\cong\mathbb{R}^2$ each passing through at least two distinct points of $M$.

**Key identity (line equation).** For distinct $a,b\in M$, a point $z\in\mathbb{C}$ lies on the line through $a$ and $b$ if and only if $\frac{z-a}{b-a}\in\mathbb{R}$, equivalently
$$
(z-a)(\bar b-\bar a)=(\bar z-\bar a)(b-a).
$$
Rearranging gives a linear equation in $z$ and $\bar z$:
$$
(\bar b-\bar a)\,z-(b-a)\,\bar z=a\bar b-\bar a\,b. \tag{$\ast$}
$$

**Solving the system.** Take two lines $\ell_1,\ell_2\in L(M)$, where $\ell_1$ passes through distinct $a,b\in M$ and $\ell_2$ passes through distinct $c,d\in M$. Their intersection $z$ satisfies both line equations. Set
$$
p=\bar b-\bar a,\quad q=b-a,\quad r=a\bar b-\bar a\,b,\qquad p'=\bar d-\bar c,\quad q'=d-c,\quad r'=c\bar d-\bar c\,d.
$$
Since $M$ is closed under conjugation and is a field, all of $p,q,r,p',q',r'\in M$. The system is
$$
pz-q\bar z=r,\qquad p'z-q'\bar z=r'.
$$
By Cramer's rule (treating $z,\bar z$ as independent unknowns),
$$
z=\frac{q\,r'-r\,q'}{q\,p'-p\,q'}. \tag{$\dagger$}
$$

**The denominator is nonzero.** Note $p=\bar q$ and $p'=\bar{q'}$, so
$$
q\,p'-p\,q'=q\,\bar{q'}-\bar q\,q'.
$$
This vanishes if and only if $q\,\bar{q'}=\bar q\,q'$, i.e., $q'/q\in\mathbb{R}$ (when $q\neq 0$, which holds since $a\neq b$). But $q'/q\in\mathbb{R}$ is precisely the condition that the direction vectors $q=b-a$ and $q'=d-c$ are real multiples of each other, i.e., the two lines are parallel (or identical). Since $z$ is *the* (unique) intersection point of $\ell_1$ and $\ell_2$, the lines are neither parallel nor identical, so $q\,p'-p\,q'\neq 0$.

**Conclusion.** The numerator $q\,r'-r\,q'\in M$ and the denominator $q\,p'-p\,q'\in M\setminus\{0\}$. Since $M$ is a field (closed under addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division by nonzero elements),
$$
z=\frac{q\,r'-r\,q'}{q\,p'-p\,q'}\in M.
$$

Therefore the intersection of any two lines from $L(M)$ lies in $M$. $\blacksquare$

